PART 8: KEY CONCEPTS REVIEWED

================================================================================

Concept 1: JSON Objects

JSON = JavaScript Object Notation

It is a standard format for exchanging structured data and is easy to read and parse.

Example:

{
  "message": "https://images.dog.ceo/breeds/retriever-golden/n02099601_1003.jpg",
  "status": "success"
}


Accessing properties:

Code         | Returns
-------------|--------------------------------
data.message | Dog image URL
data.status  | API response status


Easy memory:

Object.Property

data.message
     ↓
Get the "message" property from data


================================================================================

Concept 2: Dynamic Attributes

================================================================================

Dynamic attributes get their values from variables or state.

Example:

<img src={imageUrl} />

Attribute | Dynamic Value | Purpose
----------|---------------|---------------------------------
src       | imageUrl      | Uses state as image source


Flow:

imageUrl Changes
      ↓
React Re-renders
      ↓
src Changes
      ↓
New Image Appears


No manual page refresh is required.

================================================================================

Concept 3: Conditional Rendering with &&

================================================================================

Example:

{imageUrl && <img src={imageUrl} />}

Condition          | Result
-------------------|--------------------------
imageUrl has value | Display the image
imageUrl is empty  | Display nothing


This prevents an empty/broken image from being displayed before an image URL is available.

================================================================================

Concept 4: Error Handling

================================================================================

Error handling catches and manages failures gracefully.

Block   | Purpose
--------|-----------------------------------------------
try     | Run code that may fail
catch   | Handle the error
finally | Always execute cleanup/final operations


Example:

try {

  // Code that might fail

} catch (error) {

  // Handle the error

} finally {

  // Always runs
}


Purpose:

Catch Errors
     ↓
Prevent Normal Flow from Breaking
     ↓
Display Helpful Error Message


================================================================================

Concept 5: Loading State

================================================================================

A loading state tracks whether data is currently being fetched.

loading | Button Text        | Button State
--------|--------------------|---------------
false   | Get Random Puppy   | Enabled
true    | Loading...         | Disabled


Example:

{loading ? 'Loading...' : 'Get Random Puppy'}


This uses the ternary operator:

condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse


Applied here:

loading = true
→ "Loading..."

loading = false
→ "Get Random Puppy"


Loading state provides feedback to the user and helps prevent repeated requests.

